                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "4cb594a4-f27b-4e22-8638-fc7e294e3f41",
                        "source_entry_id": "fbc1ba41-04f6-3a78-9c6f-5abecfd0a649",
                        "source_sense_id": "8f9e4914-de1e-3841-bcbf-df04c7792328",
                        "target_lang_word": "waif",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "waif",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "667069d7-c648-3826-8c69-1a0203e16a2d",
                                        "entry_id": "06277894-8563-3392-ac2f-5f6df7e0206c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An article of movable property which has been found, and of which the owner is not known, such as goods washed up on a beach or thrown away by an absconding thief; such items belong to the Crown, which may grant the right of ownership to them to a lord of a manor."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "British",
                                            "archaic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "831f080a-3bfa-466d-aa33-a891087d0795",
                                                "sense_id": "667069d7-c648-3826-8c69-1a0203e16a2d",
                                                "text": "waifs and strays"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "46a4d654-b753-3fd5-a880-38c5bdfabc83",
                                        "entry_id": "06277894-8563-3392-ac2f-5f6df7e0206c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Something found, especially if without an owner; something which comes along, as it were, by chance."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2bc6d4d7-7b78-3f16-aa63-378e9ca4bedb",
                                        "entry_id": "06277894-8563-3392-ac2f-5f6df7e0206c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A person (especially a child) who is homeless and without means of support; also, a person excluded from society; an outcast."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4a7472bf-25d2-4780-a5c2-2832878e9766",
                                                "sense_id": "2bc6d4d7-7b78-3f16-aa63-378e9ca4bedb",
                                                "text": "But vvhat a vvretched, and diſconſolate Hermitage is that Houſe, vvhich is not viſited by thee [God], and vvhat a VVayue, and Stray is that Man, that hath not thy Markes vpon him?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6f49b21f-f2ec-4f87-bd21-87571b0d7c42",
                                                "sense_id": "2bc6d4d7-7b78-3f16-aa63-378e9ca4bedb",
                                                "text": "Tenderly Kala nursed her little waif, wondering silently why it did not gain strength and agility as did the little apes of other mothers. It was nearly a year from the time the little fellow came into her possession before he would walk alone, and as for climbing—my, but how stupid he was!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6ba14d70-3aa5-4e78-af34-f60c2785125e",
                                                "sense_id": "2bc6d4d7-7b78-3f16-aa63-378e9ca4bedb",
                                                "text": "Only an old woman, bloated, disheveled and bleared. / Far-wandered waif of other days, / Huddles for sleep in a doorway, / Homeless."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d47244c1-ca9a-382e-b616-87db91f841f2",
                                        "entry_id": "06277894-8563-3392-ac2f-5f6df7e0206c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A very thin person."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly",
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "34ceb88f-e992-472f-bc13-fdc17b2c9210",
                                                "sense_id": "d47244c1-ca9a-382e-b616-87db91f841f2",
                                                "text": "When we had done all the things there were to do, he passed out and I clung to his reassuringly solid, soft stomach—paternal, so different to the indie waifs—and cried."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9ba6a2a9-cb63-33b4-9520-1134aca62520",
                                        "entry_id": "06277894-8563-3392-ac2f-5f6df7e0206c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A plant introduced in a place outside its native range but not persistently naturalized."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly",
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "waif",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3ff5aeee-7b1d-3849-94b0-f9a4f44575c2",
                                        "entry_id": "17fcfd68-f4c5-3f96-87b9-0a7f937f6921",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To cast aside or reject, and thus make a waif."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e44a6cfc-aad0-4084-9b35-9c2d55d34e2b",
                                                "sense_id": "3ff5aeee-7b1d-3849-94b0-f9a4f44575c2",
                                                "text": "It is true that Guy, Count of Ponthieu, holds fief under me, but I have no control over the laws of his realm. And by those laws, he hath right of life and death over all stranded and waifed on his coast."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "waif",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0d23a091-e124-35d7-affa-2768850c7879",
                                        "entry_id": "c3762a47-88f6-3ca3-84dc-1bba6dc319da",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A small flag used as a signal."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "50b3c55b-607c-4b96-84b7-6fdd32efe1f0",
                                                "sense_id": "0d23a091-e124-35d7-affa-2768850c7879",
                                                "text": "[page 440] The allusion to waifs and waif-poles in the last chapter but one, necessitates some account of the laws and regulations of the whale fishery, of which the waif may be deemed the grand symbol and badge. […] [page 441] [A] fish is technically fast when it bears a waif, or any other recognised symbol of possession; so long as the party waifing it plainly evince their ability at any time to take it alongside, as well as their intention to do so."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "waif",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8fb1619d-79df-39d1-8290-b6a3c9840ce4",
                                        "entry_id": "003c9a98-da28-3ffa-9f60-81ba9a6879fa",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Something (such as clouds or smoke) carried aloft by the wind."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "9010226e-88c8-4ab0-bfdd-c3fcdf92684d",
                        "source_entry_id": "fbc1ba41-04f6-3a78-9c6f-5abecfd0a649",
                        "source_sense_id": "8f9e4914-de1e-3841-bcbf-df04c7792328",
                        "target_lang_word": "changeling",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "changeling",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "fd1bd324-d341-352e-b574-8087c2e9eefd",
                                        "entry_id": "d7bb5c00-baf7-3c71-89be-23086c69d336",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "In pre-modern European folklore: an infant of a magical creature that was secretly exchanged for a human infant. In British, Irish and Scandinavian mythology the exchanged infants were thought to be those of fairies, sprites or trolls; in other places, they were ascribed to demons, devils, or witches."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "European",
                                            "also",
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "374fabc4-b833-475d-8a9c-816cdb9aa4f5",
                                                "sense_id": "fd1bd324-d341-352e-b574-8087c2e9eefd",
                                                "text": "[S]he, as her attendant, hath / A louely boy ſtollen, from an Indian king: / She neuer had ſo ſweete a changeling."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f9b49ed8-f1c0-4479-8668-82a5a298bd62",
                                                "sense_id": "fd1bd324-d341-352e-b574-8087c2e9eefd",
                                                "text": "Every Lover admires his miſtris, though ſhee be very deformed of her ſelfe, [...] her feet ſtinke, ſhee breed lice, a meere changeling, a very monſter, [...]: if he loue her once, he admires her for all this, he takes no notice of any ſuch errors, or imperfections of body or minde."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3d1a76d9-f76a-49e9-bfde-b59a5c417a2f",
                                                "sense_id": "fd1bd324-d341-352e-b574-8087c2e9eefd",
                                                "text": "Juſt as a Changeling ſeems below the reſt / Of Men, or rather is a two-legg’d Beaſt, / So theſe Gigantick Souls amaz'd we find / As much above the reſt of human kind."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2a6ed661-09bf-4979-b7f7-9d6eb5a18f88",
                                                "sense_id": "fd1bd324-d341-352e-b574-8087c2e9eefd",
                                                "text": "Nothing will induce parents to show any attention to a child that they suspect of being a changeling."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3bf3b227-7b41-4190-8091-dbe7e0ff28aa",
                                                "sense_id": "fd1bd324-d341-352e-b574-8087c2e9eefd",
                                                "text": "It was easy enough to see that the child was a changeling, for it wasn't like other children at all it screamed and cried, as if a knife stuck in it, and it wheezed and hit about with its arms like a huldre-cat, and was as ugly as sin."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "69419141-ded6-4b72-b787-6fb8fc182388",
                                                "sense_id": "fd1bd324-d341-352e-b574-8087c2e9eefd",
                                                "text": "His nurse had told him all about changelings, and how the little people would always try to steal a beautiful human child out of its cradle and put in its stead one of their own ailing, puking brats [...]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "935179a4-92d0-301c-8196-5ddaf04bcdf1",
                                        "entry_id": "d7bb5c00-baf7-3c71-89be-23086c69d336",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A person or object (especially when regarded as inferior) secretly exchanged for something else."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6d8dc8c4-b9f5-4e13-8c5a-189c5dfbeee7",
                                                "sense_id": "935179a4-92d0-301c-8196-5ddaf04bcdf1",
                                                "text": "When I firſt read Mr. Walker’s Circular Letter to the Arch-deacons, I fancied he intended to give us an Account of thoſe worthy Men, [...] But after about ten Years going with this Work, and that the Time of its Birth was come, I ſoon found it to be a perfect Changeling. Inſtead of what I expected it to be, I ſaw a huge Heap of the moſt Heterogeneous Characters, [...] a great many of them much more deſerving to have their Names blotted out, than their Memory to be preſerved."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "98bd2082-7855-38b8-a355-979ee100d352",
                                        "entry_id": "d7bb5c00-baf7-3c71-89be-23086c69d336",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An infant secretly exchanged with another infant deliberately or by mistake; a swapling."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly",
                                            "informal",
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "12f20d20-96b7-3c33-b052-c2b30d079835",
                                        "entry_id": "d7bb5c00-baf7-3c71-89be-23086c69d336",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An organism which can change shape to mimic others; a shape-shifter."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "48b5088f-d37d-31f1-bdcd-2da091e8b3dd",
                                        "entry_id": "d7bb5c00-baf7-3c71-89be-23086c69d336",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A person apt to change their loyalty or thinking; a waverer."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "also",
                                            "archaic",
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f65b6f87-f97a-43f8-a4f6-fb16cd38521b",
                                                "sense_id": "48b5088f-d37d-31f1-bdcd-2da091e8b3dd",
                                                "text": "To face the garment of rebellion / With ſome fine colour that may pleaſe the eye / Of fickle changlings and poore diſcontents, / Which gape and rub the elbow at the newes / Of hurly-burly innouation, [...]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "20433958-6329-4481-aea1-92097503188e",
                                                "sense_id": "48b5088f-d37d-31f1-bdcd-2da091e8b3dd",
                                                "text": "Fal[staff]. [...] If you will lodge with me in Eaſtcheap, you ſhall ſee the thouſand pound fairly ſpent in ſack: you ſhall ſhare with me to the utmoſt farthing. But for dry reſtitution, I have not been accustom'd to it of many years. You would not have me a changeling at this time of day, I hope, Maſter Shallow. / Shal[low]. Changeling! no, Sir John, thou art no changeling; but, depend on it, I will not put up with this wrong. [...] I will have my money; depend on't I will have my money."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7e96c253-0fd5-4866-966f-a2308a00cad4",
                                                "sense_id": "48b5088f-d37d-31f1-bdcd-2da091e8b3dd",
                                                "text": "What words are these have fall’n from me?\n⁠Can calm despair and wild unrest\n⁠Be tenants of a single breast,\nOr sorrow such a changeling be?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5330fa7b-84b8-3949-ae0c-2dfa9903256d",
                                        "entry_id": "d7bb5c00-baf7-3c71-89be-23086c69d336",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An idiot, a simpleton."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9fd2d025-c2f2-4fd1-8507-b3da1f740a94",
                                                "sense_id": "5330fa7b-84b8-3949-ae0c-2dfa9903256d",
                                                "text": "They alſo obſerue Lunaticks and changelings, and the Coniurer writeth downe their ſayings in a booke, groueling on the ground, as if he whiſperd to the Deuill to tell him the truth, and ſo expoundeth the letter, as it were by inſpiration."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "88cea938-43af-4ec7-a88b-da006c3de182",
                                                "sense_id": "5330fa7b-84b8-3949-ae0c-2dfa9903256d",
                                                "text": "We wander in the Fields of Air below: / Changlings and Fooles of Heav'n: and thence ſhut out, / Wildly we roam in diſcontent about: [...]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "80b71835-ee06-42d8-aba4-6aa44a09ec44",
                                                "sense_id": "5330fa7b-84b8-3949-ae0c-2dfa9903256d",
                                                "text": "[C]an there be any one Sort of Men that enjoy themſelves better than thoſe which we call Idiots, Changelings, Fools, and Naturals? It may perhaps ſound harſh, but upon due Conſideration it will be found abundantly true, that theſe Perſons in all Circumſtances fare beſt, and live moſt comfortably: [...]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "changeling",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "80bf5aed-6ea5-3354-aed3-ed41d681e062",
                                        "entry_id": "7eaaeafb-1368-3386-90c9-d80ef5f9b85b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Changeable, fickle, inconstant, wavering."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "archaic",
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3fb0f95b-54e7-44f8-a5e0-2ae481b47dfb",
                                                "sense_id": "80bf5aed-6ea5-3354-aed3-ed41d681e062",
                                                "text": "Away thou changeling motley humouriſt, / Leave me, and in this ſtanding wooden cheſt, / Conſorted with theſe few books, let me lye / In priſon, and here be coffin'd, when I dye."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
